","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the standard enthalpies of formation (ΔHf°) and the standard entropies (S°) of the products and reactants. The standard enthalpy of formation is the entropy change when one mole of a compound is formed from its elements in their standard states. The standard entropy is the entropy of a substance in its standard state.

The reaction is:
3 CO(g) + O3(g) → 3 CO2(g)

Given that the reaction is performed at constant pressure and a temperature of 298 K, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣnS°(products) - ΣnS°(reactants)

First, we need to find the standard entropies (S°) of CO, O2, and CO2 at 298 K. These values can be taken from standard standard thermodynamic data tables:

S°(CO(g)) = 170.9 J/mol·K
S'(O2(g)) = 205.14 J/mol·K
S'(O3(g)) = 236.7 J/mol·K
S°(CO2(g)) = 213.7 J/mol·K

Now, we can calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction:

ΔS = 3 * S°(CO2(g)) - [3 * S°(CO(g)) + S'(O3(g)))]
ΔS = 3 * (213.7 J/mol·K) - [3 * (170.9 J/mol·K) + 236.7 J/mol·K]
ΔS = 641.1 J/mol·K - [512.7 J/mol·K + 236.7 J/mol·K]
ΔS = 641.1 J/mol·K - 749.4 J/mol·K
ΔS = -108.3 J/mol·K

So, the change in entropy for the reaction is -108.3 J/K.",fluency,"# Task Description
