Substituting these into the curl formula:
\[
\text{curl} \, \mathbf{V} = \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial z} \mathbf{i} + \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial V_z}{\partial y} \mathbf{J} + \frac{\partial V_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x}{\partial y} \mathbf{k} = \frac{\partial 0}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} \mathbf{i} + \frac{\partial P}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial 0}{\partial y} \mathbf{j} + \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \mathbf{k}.
\]
Since \(V_z = 0\), the terms \(\frac{\partial V_z}{\partial x}\) and \(\frac{\partial V_z}{\partial y}\) are zero. Since the vector field is only a 2D vector field, the partial derivatives with respect to \(z\) are zero, so \(\frac{\partial P}{\partial z} = 0\) and \(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial z} = 0\). Thus, the curl simplifies to:
\[
\text{curl} \, \mathbf{V} = 0 \mathbf{i} + 0 \mathbf{j} + \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \mathbf{k} = \left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Thus, the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is \(\left( \frac{\partial Q}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}\).

